Validation Reference

Validation Objective

Demonstrate that the execution capability of AVIA successfully transforms approved adaptive decisions into vehicle behavior that fulfills the intended operational purpose under representative conditions.

---

Validation Methods

Validation shall be performed through one or more of the following methods:

Method| Purpose
Operational Evaluation| Assess whether executed actions produce the intended vehicle behavior in representative operational situations.
Scenario-Based Assessment| Evaluate execution across normal, changing, uncertain, degraded, and failure conditions.
Comparative Assessment| Compare resulting vehicle behavior against the intended behavior represented by the approved decision.
Stakeholder Acceptance| Confirm that the execution capability fulfills its intended operational role within the AVIA architecture.

---

Validation Success Criteria

Validation shall demonstrate that:

- Approved decisions result in corresponding vehicle behavior.
- Executed actions remain consistent with the approved decision.
- Vehicle behavior remains within applicable operational and engineering constraints.
- Execution deviations are detectable and appropriately represented.
- Execution failures are detectable and appropriately reported.
- Execution-state information provides sufficient representation of the actual execution condition.
- The execution capability fulfills its intended role within the adaptive intelligence architecture.

---

Operational Validation Boundary

Validation shall evaluate whether execution produces the intended operational result.

It shall not independently validate:

- Whether the original operating context was interpreted correctly.
- Whether the generated decision was the best possible decision.
- Whether the decision was properly authorized.
- Whether a separate authority mechanism correctly granted permission.

Those questions belong to their respective architectural capabilities.

---

Decision-to-Action Validation

Validation shall preserve the distinction between the intended decision and its execution:

Approved Decision
       ↓
Intended Action
       ↓
Actual Vehicle Behavior

Validation shall determine whether the actual behavior adequately fulfills the approved decision under the applicable operating conditions.

---

Execution Failure Validation

Validation shall examine representative conditions in which an approved action:

- Cannot be completed.
- Is only partially completed.
- Deviates from the intended execution.
- Becomes impractical because of changing conditions.
- Produces an execution state requiring further system response.

The purpose is to establish whether the execution architecture behaves appropriately under such conditions.

Determination of the subsequent adaptive response remains outside this objective unless explicitly assigned through later architectural development.

---

Execution-State Validation

Validation shall assess whether execution-state information is sufficiently meaningful to support downstream system behavior.

This includes determining whether the system can distinguish relevant conditions such as:

Executing
   ↓
Completed

or

Executing
   ↓
Partial

or

Executing
   ↓
Deviation

or

Executing
   ↓
Failure

The final execution-state model shall be established through derived system-specific requirements.

---

Authority Validation Boundary

A successful execution shall not be treated as evidence that authorization was correct.

The architectural distinction remains:

Decision
   ↓
Authorization
   ↓
Approved Decision
   ↓
Execution

Validation of authorization shall belong to the future authority architecture.

